      "notes": [
        {
          "on": "idgt",
          "tone": "info",
          "title": "The 2023 trust shares Maya’s cap, usually by design.",
          "body": "It is a grantor trust, so its gain counts against Maya’s $10M. Founders use these to move future growth out of their estate; it adds no QSBS room. Whether to change that before a sale is a question for tax counsel."
        },
        {
          "on": "adatrust",
          "tone": "info",
          "title": "Ada’s and Theo’s trusts each get their own cap.",
          "body": "Gifted QSBS keeps its status and holding period. The IRS can merge trusts that share a grantor and substantially the same beneficiaries when cutting tax was a principal purpose. Art. 4 of each names one different child; the remainder terms need the same check. Their separate status rests on the counsel memo, and a later amendment, swap power or loan could change it."
        },
        {
          "on": "opco",
          "tone": "ask",
          "title": "Four conditions aren’t in the documents.",
          "body": "The $50M asset test, the active-business test, the excluded-field test and the redemption rules are assumed here (see the checklist). Company counsel confirms them before anyone counts on the exclusion."
        }
